\b\i\fs24 Retirement Plan\par
\b0\i0\fs18\tab\par
\b\fs20 Assumptions:\b0\fs18\par
Our detail plans are based on the following assumptions:\par
You expect to retire when you are [\cf1 Ret_Age\cf0 ] years of age\par
Your desired income during your retirement years is $[\cf1 Ret_Inc\cf0 ] \par
\par
\b\fs20 Retirement Income Shortfall\b0\fs18\par
The Retirement Income Shortfall is the difference between the amount you will be making and the amount you will be spending at retirement. \par
This amount is $[\cf1 RetInc_Shortfall\cf0 ] and the capital required to fund it is [\cf1 Ret_CapRequired\cf0 ] \par
[If (\cf1 RetInc_Shortfall\cf0   > 0)]\par
Your total value of your investments at retirement of  $[\cf1 RetBal_All\cf0 ] are sufficient to cover the income shortfall. \par
[\cf1 Endif\cf0 ]\par
[If (\cf1 RetInc_Shortfall\cf0  < 0)]\par
Your total value of your investments at retirement of  $[\cf1 RetBal_All\cf0 ] are NOT sufficient to cover the income shortfall. \par
[\cf1 Endif\cf0 ]\par
[If (\cf1 RetInc_Shortfall\cf0   = 0)]\par
Your total value of your investments at retirement of  $[\cf1 RetBal_All\cf0 ] are sufficient to fund the income shortfall. \par
[\cf1 Endif\cf0 ]\par
\tab\par
It is important to note that in order to support your desired income goal of $[\cf1 Ret_IncDesired\cf0 ]  and the stated Life Events, you will need to withdraw $[\cf1 RetInc_Shortfall\cf0 ] from your investments on a yearly basis during your retirement years. In such cases the balance of your investments will be reduced by the amount needed.\par
Such withdrawals can deplete the corresponding investment balance during retirement or even turn it into a deficit (negative balance). In the latter case, you will not be able to sustain your desired lifestyle.\par
\par
